#' Self-regulated-learning (MSLQ) construct scores simulated by large language models
#'
#' Five data sets of Motivated Strategies for Learning Questionnaire (MSLQ)
#' construct scores, each a random sample of 300 cases generated by one large
#' language model in the study *"Delving into the psychology of Machines:
#' Exploring the structure of self-regulated learning via LLM-generated survey
#' responses"* (Computers in Human Behavior, 2025). One data set per model:
#' `SRL_GPT`, `SRL_Gemini`, `SRL_Claude`, `SRL_Mistral`, and `SRL_LLaMa`.
#'
#' Only the five models whose responses carry estimable structure are included.
#' Two other generators from the original study (ChatGPT and LeChat) produced
#' near-independent items (mean absolute inter-item correlation
#' \eqn{\approx 0.03}), so their network is the empty graph; they are omitted.
#' The five retained models span the spectrum the paper describes, from
#' realistically structured (`SRL_GPT`) to strongly "over-coherent"
#' (`SRL_Gemini`, `SRL_Claude`).
#'
#' @format Each is a data frame with 300 rows and 5 variables -- the MSLQ
#'   construct scores (each the mean of that construct's Likert items, range
#'   1--7):
#' \describe{
#'   \item{`CSU`}{cognitive strategy use}
#'   \item{`IV`}{intrinsic value}
#'   \item{`SE`}{self-efficacy}
#'   \item{`SR`}{self-regulation}
#'   \item{`TA`}{test anxiety}
#' }
#' @source Saqr, M. (2025). Delving into the psychology of Machines: Exploring
#'   the structure of self-regulated learning via LLM-generated survey responses.
#'   *Computers in Human Behavior*, 173, 108769. \doi{10.1016/j.chb.2025.108769}
#' @examples
#' # partial-correlation network of the five constructs for one model
#' net <- ebic_glasso(SRL_GPT)
#' net
#' net_centralities(net)
#' @name SRL
#' @keywords datasets
"SRL_GPT"

#' @rdname SRL
#' @format NULL
"SRL_Gemini"

#' @rdname SRL
#' @format NULL
"SRL_Claude"

#' @rdname SRL
#' @format NULL
"SRL_Mistral"

#' @rdname SRL
#' @format NULL
"SRL_LLaMa"

#' Synthetic weekly self-regulated-learning panel
#'
#' A synthetic panel of 100 students measured in four consecutive weeks on five
#' self-regulated-learning indicators -- a small, fully shareable data set for
#' the panel graphical VAR ([panel_gvar()]).
#'
#' The data were simulated, once, from a stationary random-intercept VAR(1)
#' (the panel graphical VAR model) whose structure was taken from the weekly
#' means of real experience-sampling data (the anonymized `esm_srl` data of the
#' idiographic package): the contemporaneous partial correlations, the
#' temporal pattern (autoregressive effects and an effort -> efficacy lagged
#' effect, scaled to 0.6 of the estimated values so trait and state differences
#' are separable in four waves), and the correlations among students' average
#' levels, with 60% of each variable's variance between students. Values are on
#' a 0-100 scale (one of the 2,000 values was bounded to the scale) and rounded
#' to one decimal. No real student is represented.
#'
#' @format A data frame with 400 rows (100 students x 4 weeks) and 7 columns:
#' \describe{
#'   \item{`student`}{Student identifier, `"S001"` to `"S100"`.}
#'   \item{`week`}{Week of measurement, 1 to 4.}
#'   \item{`efficacy`}{Self-efficacy.}
#'   \item{`planning`}{Planning.}
#'   \item{`effort`}{Effort regulation.}
#'   \item{`enjoyment`}{Enjoyment.}
#'   \item{`anxiety`}{Anxiety.}
#' }
#' @source Simulated; structure derived from the weekly means of the `esm_srl`
#'   data in the idiographic package.
#' @examples
#' head(srl_panel)
#' fit <- panel_gvar(srl_panel,
#'                   vars = c("efficacy", "planning", "effort", "enjoyment",
#'                            "anxiety"),
#'                   id = "student", wave = "week")
#' summary(fit)
"srl_panel"
